##
    FizzBuzz, the classic first exercise.

    A fair look at the language's control flow: a range loop that reads as a
    sentence, an if/else-if chain with no parentheses around the conditions, and a function
    that yields a string.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        loop for n = 1 to 20
            Console.WriteLine(Program.Describe(n));
        end loop
    end function

    ##
        @summary: Names a number by what divides it.

        @remarks: Divisibility by 15 has to be tested first. Testing it last would never be
        reached, because 15 is divisible by 3 and the chain stops at the first branch that
        matches.

        @n: the number to describe.
        @yields: "FizzBuzz", "Fizz", "Buzz", or the number written out.
    ##
    string function Describe(integer n)
        if n % 15 == 0
            yield "FizzBuzz";
        else if n % 3 == 0
            yield "Fizz";
        else if n % 5 == 0
            yield "Buzz";
        end if

        yield n.ToString();
    end function

end model
